   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## §7b — Actually Using Both T4s (`accelerate launch`)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Important correction:** the §7 cell above runs inside this notebook's single Jupyter kernel process. `Accelerator()` called that way always creates a **world_size = 1** group — it uses exactly one of your two T4s — no matter what `accelerate_config.yaml` says. The YAML only takes effect when `accelerate launch` is the thing that *starts* the Python process: it's a process launcher that spawns N independent processes (one per GPU) and only then does each of those processes see itself as \"rank i of N\". A cell running inside an already-started kernel can't retroactively split itself into two processes.\n",
    "\n",
    "So: §7 above is fine for a quick single-GPU smoke test inside the notebook, but for the real runs (the ones your Δslope significance numbers should come from), use the two cells below instead. They (1) write everything you've already defined out to a real `.py` file, then (2) launch it with `accelerate launch`, which is what actually gets you 2 processes on 2 GPUs.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two other fixes bundled into the exported script vs. the inline §7 cell above (worth knowing about even if you keep using §7 for quick tests):\n",
    "- **Baseline-path fix**: §7 computed `baseline_pass_at_k.json` at `run_dir.parent.parent`, which strips *both* `seed` and `model_size` from the path. Since `EXP_NAME` is the same string for both model sizes (only the `model_size` subfolder differs), this meant a 0.5B run and a 1.5B run of the same gate type would read/write the *same* baseline file — corrupting exactly the cross-scale (N1/N3) comparison. Fixed to `run_dir.parent` (strips only `seed`, correctly keeping `model_size` separate) below.\n",
    "- **Peft-native checkpoint**: the exported script also calls `save_pretrained()` on every checkpoint, in addition to `accelerator.save_state()`. `accelerator.save_state()`'s on-disk format for a `PeftModel` isn't guaranteed identical to peft's own adapter format across every `accelerate` version, so §11 (interference) and any `PeftModel.from_pretrained(...)` calls should point at the `.../adapter` subfolder, not `.../accelerate_ckpt`.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
